WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.420
Where does a protein go inside a living cell?

2
00:00:03.740 --> 00:00:05.300
Look at this branch of a nerve cell.

3
00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:11.020
Those green dots mark places we can see because a protein carries its own

4
00:00:11.020 --> 00:00:12.020
fluorescent tag.

5
00:00:13.833 --> 00:00:19.533
Green fluorescent protein, or GFP, gives off green light when blue light shines on

6
00:00:19.533 --> 00:00:23.113
it. Join its gene to another protein's gene.

7
00:00:24.353 --> 00:00:29.153
The cell makes the other protein with GFP attached, so green light marks its

8
00:00:29.153 --> 00:00:30.153
location.

9
00:00:31.517 --> 00:00:33.657
Here the tagged protein is gephyrin.

10
00:00:34.877 --> 00:00:40.417
It forms a scaffold beneath inhibitory synapses, the places where one cell can quiet

11
00:00:40.417 --> 00:00:46.817
another. Pull back and the tags reveal rows of bright dots along the nerve cell's

12
00:00:46.817 --> 00:00:47.977
branching extensions.

13
00:00:49.967 --> 00:00:55.367
A fluorescent protein can show where something is or report a signal that changes.

14
00:00:56.887 --> 00:00:59.187
Calcium acts as a messenger inside cells.

15
00:01:00.447 --> 00:01:05.947
One calcium sensor links cyan and yellow fluorescent proteins at opposite ends.

16
00:01:07.227 --> 00:01:12.007
Between them sit a calcium-binding protein called calmodulin and a short piece it

17
00:01:12.007 --> 00:01:13.007
can grip.

18
00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:19.660
When calcium rises, it binds calmodulin, which grips that short piece and pulls the

19
00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:22.900
ends close. Shine light that excites cyan.

20
00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:29.440
With yellow close by, some energy passes from cyan to yellow, making yellow brighter

21
00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:30.440
and cyan dimmer.

22
00:01:32.233 --> 00:01:36.393
To track the change, divide the yellow brightness by the cyan brightness.

23
00:01:37.713 --> 00:01:40.193
This ratio rises when calcium rises.

24
00:01:41.493 --> 00:01:45.473
If calcium drops and the ends move apart, what should happen to the ratio?

25
00:01:52.033 --> 00:01:55.633
As calcium drops, the grip loosens and the ends separate.

26
00:01:57.033 --> 00:01:59.253
Less energy passes from cyan to yellow.

27
00:02:00.673 --> 00:02:05.673
Yellow gives off less light relative to cyan, so yellow brightness divided by cyan

28
00:02:05.673 --> 00:02:10.993
brightness falls. The readout follows how much the sensor's ends come together.

29
00:02:12.867 --> 00:02:16.427
For a location measurement, the tag itself can get in the way.

30
00:02:17.687 --> 00:02:20.287
Some proteins carry an address at their very end.

31
00:02:21.447 --> 00:02:24.707
That address sends them to compartments called peroxisomes.

32
00:02:25.887 --> 00:02:27.847
A tag placed after it can hide it.

33
00:02:29.517 --> 00:02:33.397
To guard against that, put the tag where the address stays exposed.

34
00:02:34.717 --> 00:02:37.917
Then use a marker that shows where the peroxisomes are.

35
00:02:39.137 --> 00:02:42.717
Check whether the tagged protein and the marker light up the same spots.

36
00:02:44.617 --> 00:02:50.197
Miyawaki, Tsien and colleagues published that color-shifting calcium sensor, called

37
00:02:50.197 --> 00:02:52.137
cameleon, in 1997.

38
00:02:53.777 --> 00:02:58.697
Its shifting balance of yellow and cyan gave researchers a way to follow calcium

39
00:02:58.697 --> 00:03:04.617
inside a cell. Fluorescent tags can also measure protein amounts in single cells.

40
00:03:04.617 --> 00:03:05.597
Fluorescent tags can also measure protein amounts in single cells.

41
00:03:06.550 --> 00:03:12.290
In 2015, Kurt Schmoller, Jan Skotheim and colleagues tagged a protein called Whi5.

42
00:03:13.570 --> 00:03:15.610
They measured it in single living cells.

43
00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:22.050
At birth, small daughter cells had about as many Whi5 molecules as larger cells.

44
00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:26.210
In less space, that made Whi5 more concentrated.
